UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 1: Story elements: character, setting and plot', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Recognise how characters, settings and connected events create a complete narrative.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. In \"The koala waited for rain\", name the character and one likely goal.\nCOMPARE: The koala is the character; it may need water or cooler weather. The goal is an inference, not a fact unless the text says so.\n2. Find a place and time clue in \"At midnight, lanterns glowed outside the campsite\".\nCOMPARE: At midnight is the time; outside the campsite locates the scene. The lanterns contribute atmosphere.\n3. Explain why this event follows the first: \"A storm snapped the tent rope. Dad grabbed the spare rope.\"\nCOMPARE: Dad acts because the storm damaged the rope; the second event responds to the problem.\n4. Identify character, setting and connected events: \"At the fair, Anya dropped her ticket but found it under a bench.\"\nCOMPARE: Anya is the character; the fair is the setting; she loses then finds her ticket. The loss causes the search.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nRead this original text: \"At the town garden, Noor planted a sunflower seed. A curious crow uncovered it. Noor placed smooth stones around the pot, and the seed stayed safe.\" Identify the character, place, problem, response and ending; quote one phrase as evidence for each. Write a new four-sentence story where the setting changes what the character must do.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Recognise how characters, settings and connected events create a complete narrative.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read this original text: \"At the town garden, Noor planted a sunflower seed. A curious crow uncovered it. Noor placed smooth stones around the pot, and the seed stayed safe.\" Identify the character, place, problem, respo Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using character on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A narrator and a character are not automatically the same person.. Correction: The koala is the character; it may need water or cooler weather.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A narrator and a character are not automatically the same person. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating character on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with character, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about character with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason 